# Resumption: goal and constraints

Prepared September 17, 2026. This is a synthesis for review, not a new project
policy, sprint launch, or authorization to change thesis scope.

## Goal and completion

Produce a mathematically sound, readable MSc thesis on probing Viterbo’s
conjecture that earns Jörn’s explicit whole-thesis PASS. The project has several
related results; it does not need one forced narrative. A finished outcome also
requires supported claims, resolvable code/data/figure/certificate references,
truthful reproduction promises, and the applicable submission requirements.

A build, automated review, accepted proof, or chapter-level PASS is insufficient
for whole-thesis acceptance. The planned final advisor sequence is Jörn’s
satisfaction, Kai’s review, incorporation of feedback, then submission without
another full-version review by Kai. Archive publication is a separate closure
action, not something established by thesis acceptance.

## Operating constraints on this agent

| Constraint and authority | Consequence for the work |
| --- | --- |
| A current developer instruction prohibits spawning subagents unless the user or applicable AGENTS.md/skill instructions explicitly request delegation or parallel agent work. | This restricts autonomous parallel authoring and independent review, despite their value for the sprint. The saved kickoff recommends workers, but is not itself an exception named in that instruction. The user's comments identify this tension; they should not be misrepresented as a blanket ban originating from the user. |
| The injected tool configuration exposes 100 agent slots including the root; repository configuration sets 99 additional threads. | Capacity and permission are different. This is not evidence of a successful worker load test, unlimited API throughput, or adequate RAM for every computation. |
| This turn requests a report. | Inspect and report; do not silently launch the thesis sprint, experiments, publication, or a new goal. An editable report is within the requested work. |
| Filesystem access is unrestricted, network access is enabled, and tool approval prompts are disabled. | Technical access is not authorization for paid infrastructure, external messages, destructive changes, or changes to Jörn’s interactive layout. |
| Applicable skill instructions must be followed; local edits use apply_patch. | Skill requirements can add workflow steps. Surface any actual resulting restriction rather than inventing an approval gate. |
| Work should continue autonomously within authorized scope. | Resolve routine choices, finish necessary implementation and checks, and escalate material scope/resource decisions. Do not repeatedly seek permission already supplied. |
| Progress updates are required during sustained work. | Keep them brief; maintain the substantive report in a patchable artifact rather than repeatedly replacing it in chat. |

The delegation restriction is an instruction-level limitation, not an argument
that serial work is optimal. It matters for the production sprint; a short
recap does not itself need a team. No agents have been spawned for this report.

## Time, money, and attention

- The original recap had a 120-second limit. I failed to record its start time
  or report elapsed time. I therefore cannot substantiate compliance. The
  first explicit clock reading I took was September 17 at 06:32:32 UTC, after
  several exchanges; that cannot reconstruct the original deadline.
- The recorded second-sprint proposal is $3,000 shadow API quota, roughly six
  hours elapsed, and about 120 minutes of Jörn’s attention. It is not an
  already-started clock, a current official deadline, or paid-resource consent.
- Shadow API cost, elapsed time, and human attention are separate budgets.
  Report measured spend or explicitly unavailable accounting. The local usage
  script has coverage limits and is not a quota enforcer or attention tracker.
- The coordinator owns a scope-based delivery estimate and updates. Surface
  overruns before exhausting the envelope. Spending the budget is not success.
- Expect asynchronous replies, usually 10 seconds–10 minutes and occasionally
  an hour; reading may occupy about 30 minutes. Continue independent work while
  waiting. Do not ask Jörn to guess delivery timing.
- No current official submission deadline or submission status is established
  by the handoff. Historical deadlines and preparation budgets are not live.

## Scientific and editorial constraints

- Write for a mathematically mature master’s reader with smooth symplectic
  background. Preserve mathematical meaning while making the reader’s route
  through definitions, questions, statements, and proofs understandable.
- Preserve research and broadly the thesis structure. Retained areas include
  HKO local maximality; the pentagon side result; data science/search;
  generalized Reeb and HK2019 foundations; first-order perturbation;
  numerics/exactness; flow-graph/CH2021 methods; reproducibility; exploratory
  visualization; necessary preliminaries; and AI-use disclosure/reflection.
- Keep the factual AI disclosure distinct from research-process reflection.
  Their final length and design remain Jörn’s decisions. Visualization and
  pentagons should retain their agreed supporting/side-result roles.
- Verify each relevant relation between source literature, formal argument,
  implementation/experiment, and manuscript claim. Exact arithmetic does not
  establish a missing implication; finite evidence does not prove an
  unrestricted theorem; a compiled PDF does not validate its prose or proofs.
- Preserve theorem hypotheses and implementation boundaries. In particular,
  fixed-facet HKO local maximality is not unrestricted local maximality, and
  the conditional flow-graph theorem does not automatically certify the
  optimized production implementation.
- HKO support includes Rust generation, human-readable Sage verification, and
  written mathematical justification of the verification and its implication.
  Do not weaken central proof obligations merely because they are expensive.
- Data science must assess mathematical discovery—patterns, geometric meaning,
  conjectures and proofs—as well as high-ratio search. Do not reduce it to an
  experiment inventory or assume a purely negative outcome in advance.
- Do not infer repertoire completion or a shortfall by comparing a method-ledger
  row count with the historical approximate count of 100. The extra comparison
  of branch-aware optimization with nonsmooth alternatives must not delay
  submission even an hour.
- New research campaigns and large reorganizations need justification against
  the thesis goal. Do not automatically implement every review suggestion.
  The restricted-family ridge relation does not belong in the pentagon chapter.
- Preserve attribution, including Pro’s contributions. Verify literature
  scope and currency from primary sources; repeated reliance on one old source
  is not independent corroboration.
- Replace a poor scaffold when useful. Avoid unexplained objectives, needless
  shorthand, implementation-first framing, premature caveats, and redundant
  previews. These examples require reader judgment, not a banned-word filter.

## Authoring, review, and coordination

- A credible authoring/review workflow is part of the work, not something
  demonstrated by having review labels. Test it on actual revised passages
  using accepted HKO text and rejected passages as evidence before scaling.
- The planning record says not to request another full-thesis PASS/FAIL review
  unless a strong proxy is known and passes; no validated proxy is recorded.
  The later kickoff calls for sample calibration before final whole-thesis
  judgment. This is an unresolved readiness requirement: neither agent votes
  nor a build should be presented as having satisfied it.
- Use existing knowledge and internal checks before spending Jörn’s typing on
  known defects. Bring consequential tradeoffs, not serial sentence repairs,
  routine scheduling, or Git housekeeping. Do not repeatedly re-review
  unchanged accepted material without a reason.
- Keep the PDF fixed during human reading and identify the reviewed artifact.
  Coordinate review-link delivery; do not overwrite a served artifact.
- The intended sprint organization gives the root scope, integration, delivery,
  and attention management; bounded authors own drafting through revision and
  rendering, with independent review and explicit file ownership. Its execution
  is subject to the delegation restriction above.
- No third broad retrospective audit is required. Workflow changes should
  address a concrete failure or testable question, not become a new preparation
  campaign.

## Resources, preservation, and reproduction

- Do not silently abandon a valuable route because of resource limits. Estimate
  demand, alternatives, cost, and benefit and bring a concrete decision when
  necessary. Do not provision paid resources or change Herdr pane layout merely
  to bypass a constraint.
- Historical hardware readings are not current measurements. No binding
  hardware blocker has been established for this report or ordinary integration.
- Preserve the substantial dirty worktree, original evidence, provenance, and
  unrelated edits. Inspect producer inputs and write effects before expensive
  runs, especially full or certificate workflows that can replace evidence.
- Follow claims to their authoritative sources. Agent-written summaries and
  old plans are fallible; absence from one search or HEAD is not proof of
  absence. Preserve decisions and unfinished work across handoffs without
  promoting historical tasks into current assignments.
- Reproduction should remain usable roughly two years later, ideally through
  the final PDF. Document producer/input relationships, environment and tool
  versions, timings, original LICCA execution where relevant, and the distinction
  between byte-identical and accepted non-byte-identical reproduction.
- The accepted archive direction is living GitHub plus one curated frozen Zenodo
  thesis-support/continuation bundle. Its publication and DOI require external
  confirmation. Do not split records or add Software Heritage by default.
- The accepted license split is Apache-2.0 for project software and CC-BY-4.0
  for eligible Jörn-owned text, figures, and data. Third-party rights remain
  separate. Exclude credentials, raw session logs, genuinely private
  correspondence, unsuitable third-party material, and disposable state from
  the archive; ordinary identifying project context is not inherently private.

